  'copilotAdoption.page.countedCopilotAuditLogEveryUserHoldsNoneSkus': 'Counted from the Copilot audit log for every user who holds none of the SKUs classified as a Copilot licence. It is a count of distinct people, not of interactions.',
  'copilotAdoption.page.thisInvisibleMicrosoftOwnCopilotUsageReportsCoverLicensed': 'This is invisible in Microsoft’s own Copilot usage reports, which cover licensed users only. It is the strongest evidence of unmet demand available, because these people chose to use Copilot with no prompting and no licence.',
  'copilotAdoption.page.recommendedLicence': 'Recommended for a licence',
  'copilotAdoption.page.heavyMicrosoftUsersStrongBusinessCase': 'Heavy Microsoft 365 users with a strong business case',
  'copilotAdoption.page.unlicensedUsersRecommendedLicenceEitherAlreadyUseCopilotLeast': 'Unlicensed users recommended for a licence - either because they already use Copilot on at least {v0} distinct days without one (proven demand), or because their business-case score reached {v1} out of 100 (workload inferred).',
  'copilotAdoption.page.fourWeightedSignalsAlreadyUsingCopilotChatLicencePoints': 'Four weighted signals: already using Copilot Chat without a licence ({v0} points, the heaviest because it is evidence rather than inference), Teams collaboration ({v1}), email volume ({v2}) and document work ({v3}). Each is a capped ratio against its own target, so no single workload can carry someone over the threshold alone. Proven demand qualifies on its own because the Copilot weight sits below the score bar, so recurrent unlicensed use could otherwise never clear it while general Microsoft 365 busyness could.',
